READING — algorithm / pseudocode
================================

Companion to `reading_desc`. Language-agnostic, but named so it can be
implemented directly against the `optimal_lambda/` package.

Assumptions carried over from `reading_desc` are marked [A].


0. Data structures
------------------

    Entity      := IRI of a knowledge-graph node        # a "type" A, B, C...
    Relation    := (subject: Entity, object: Entity)    # an arrow A -> B

    Term        := Var(iri, label)                      # optimal_lambda.term
                 | App(func: Term, arg: Term)
                 | Abs(var: Var, body: Term)

    Pointer     := handle designating a node or edge of G(t).
                   Must survive graph rewriting, so it is NOT a raw node id;
                   implement as a stable id minted per graft site.

    Reading     := record {
                     graph      : Graph        # G(t), a sharing graph
                     term       : Term         # t  (kept in step with graph)
                     pointers   : set[Pointer]
                     ontologies : set[Ontology] # models in which R is valid
                     name       : optional str  # set once saved
                   }

    Ontology    := record {                    # ont(G(t), P) -- reading_desc §7
                     graph    : Graph          # contains ABSTRACTIONS
                     term     : Term
                     pointers : set[Pointer]   # corresponds elementwise to
                   }                           # the reading's pointer set

    Question    := an Abs created by the user from a reading by selecting the
                   queried entity (already implemented: app.py "Ask question"
                   -> widgets.LambdaAbstractionModal -> Abs(var=entity,
                   body=reading_term); persisted by
                   term_utils.append_lambda_term). Its type is its bound
                   variable's type, since [(lam a) t] = [a].

    Store       := saved readings, addressable by name.
    QStore      := saved questions (the lambda DB), queryable BY TYPE.


1. Typing  —  [t]
-----------------

Normative implementation already exists: `term_utils._term_type`.

    function TYPE_OF(t: Term) -> Entity:
        while t is App:
            if t.func is Abs:
                return t.func.var.iri          # [(lam a) t] = [a]
            t = t.arg                          # [app(a,b)]  = [b]
        if t is Var:
            return t.iri                       # [a]
        return UNDEFINED

    function TYPE_AT(R, p) -> Entity:
        return TYPE_OF( SUBTERM_AT(R, p) )

SUBTERM_AT(R,p) returns the subterm t1 whose subgraph G(t1) the pointer p
designates.


2. Initial step
---------------

    function INIT_FROM_ENTITY(A: Entity) -> Reading:
        a  = fresh Var of type A                 # [a] = A
        t  = a
        G  = compile_term(t)                     # optimal_lambda.compile
        p  = POINTER_TO_ROOT(G)
        return Reading(graph=G, term=t, pointers={p})

    function INIT_FROM_SAVED(R1: Reading, p: Pointer) -> (Reading, Pointer):
        require p in R1.pointers
        # Not a special mode: reading continues generically from here,
        # with R := R1 and p as the acting position.
        return (COPY_OF(R1), p)


3. Legality
-----------

DESIGN CONSTRAINT (reading_desc §3b): the reader does not know the graph in
advance. Every operand must be either (K1) an arrow incident to the type at the
current pointer, or (K2) one of the reader's own saved readings. Consequences
for this module:

  - the move menu at a pointer of type A is built from arrows INCIDENT to A
    only — never from a global entity list, and never from a path lookup;
  - there is deliberately NO "go to node / go to entity" navigation procedure.
    Adding one would breach (K1), and it is not needed: grafting saved readings
    at a pointer already yields every application shape (reading_desc §9.1);
  - (K2) enters only THROUGH a pointer, via cases 2 and 4. EVERY procedure here
    acts at one pointer; none takes two readings as co-equal operands. The
    former case 5 did, and was removed for that reason (reading_desc §4.2d).

    function LEGAL_CONTRACTION_DEPART(R, p, arrow: Relation) -> bool:
        # cases 1 and 2: reader stands at the arrow's SUBJECT
        return TYPE_AT(R, p) == arrow.subject          # invariant I4

    function LEGAL_CONTRACTION_ARRIVE(R, p, arrow: Relation) -> bool:
        # case 4: reader stands at the arrow's OBJECT
        return TYPE_AT(R, p) == arrow.object           # invariant I4

    function LEGAL_REFLECTION(R, p, B, C) -> bool:
        A = TYPE_AT(R, p)
        # [A] two relations OUT OF A are contrasted
        return EXISTS_RELATION(A, B) and EXISTS_RELATION(A, C)

EXISTS_RELATION consults the knowledge graph. Offering the user the legal
moves at a pointer of type A needs BOTH directions:

    outgoing arrows A -> X   `kg_store.get_claims_for_subject`  (cases 1, 2)
    incoming arrows X -> A   `kg_store.get_claims_for_object`   (case 4, via
                                                                TRIVIAL_READING)


4. Contraction, case 1  —  follow a relation A -> B
----------------------------------------------------

    procedure CONTRACT_RELATION(R, p, arrow = (A, B)):
        require LEGAL_CONTRACTION_DEPART(R, p, arrow)

        t1  = SUBTERM_AT(R, p)                   # [t1] == A
        b   = fresh Var of type B
        t2  = App(t1, b)                         # [t2] == B

        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=p, old=G(t1), new=G(t2))
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, t1, t2)

        # p is re-pointed at the new application node; all other pointers
        # are untouched (invariant I2).
        REBIND(p, to = node_of(t2))

        # R.pointers unchanged as a set; p now designates the App node.
        return R

    Post: TYPE_AT(R, p) == B.


5. Contraction, case 2  —  enter a saved reading A -> R1
---------------------------------------------------------

The defining property: the reader DESCENDS INTO R1. R1's pointers become live,
p is consumed by the graft, and the reading's other pointers survive:

        P1 = Pr union (P - p)

    SHARE_GRAFTED_READING := false     # [A] default: copy. see reading_desc O1

    procedure CONTRACT_READING(R, p, A: Entity, R1: Reading):
        require TYPE_AT(R, p) == A

        t1 = SUBTERM_AT(R, p)                    # [t1] == A

        if SHARE_GRAFTED_READING:
            # one G(tr1), referenced from many readings; edits propagate
            sub    = SHARED_REF(R1.graph)
            newPtr = R1.pointers                 # still valid: same graph
        else:
            # independent instance; saved R1 stays immutable
            (sub, ptrMap) = DEEP_COPY(R1.graph)
            newPtr = { ptrMap[q] for q in R1.pointers }   # rebase onto copy

        t2 = App(t1, R1.term)

        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=p, old=G(t1), new=App-node(G(t1), sub))
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, t1, t2)

        # THE DESCENT: R1's pointers become live. p is CONSUMED by the graft;
        # every other pointer of R survives (invariant I2).
        R.pointers = newPtr union (R.pointers \ {p})

        require R.pointers is non-empty                    # invariant I6
        return R

    |P| changes by -1 + |Pr|, so one step may open several new positions.
    R.term still records the whole path traversed before the descent — only
    the POSITIONS change.


5b. Case 3  —  OMITTED (subsumed by case 4)
--------------------------------------------

The former case 3 ("arrive along A -> B": prepend a fresh Var of type A) is
not a primitive. It is case 4 instantiated at the TRIVIAL READING

    TRIVIAL_READING(A) = Reading(graph    = compile_term(a),
                                 term     = a,          # a fresh Var, [a]==A
                                 pointers = {ptr(a)})

Because case 4 discards the grafted reading's pointers, the trivial reading's
pointer contributes nothing and the result is exactly the old case 3.
See §5c and reading_desc §4.2b.

Case 1 is NOT similarly reducible to case 2: case 2 ADOPTS the grafted
reading's pointers, so instantiating it at a trivial reading would leave the
pointer on the argument rather than on the new application node.


5c. Contraction, case 4  —  arrive from a saved reading R1 -> B
----------------------------------------------------------------

Dual of case 2: the saved reading goes into FUNCTION position, so the type is
unchanged and Pr is NOT adopted. Subsumes the former case 3 when R1 is
TRIVIAL_READING(A).

    procedure CONTRACT_INCOMING_READING(R, p, R1: Reading, B: Entity):
        require TYPE_AT(R, p) == B

        t1 = SUBTERM_AT(R, p)                    # [t1] == B

        if SHARE_GRAFTED_READING:
            sub = SHARED_REF(R1.graph)
        else:
            (sub, _) = DEEP_COPY(R1.graph)       # ptrMap unused: Pr discarded

        t2 = App(R1.term, t1)                    # [t2] == [t1] == B

        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=p, old=G(t1), new=App-node(sub, G(t1)))
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, t1, t2)

        REBIND(p, to = node_of(t2))
        # Pr is DISCARDED — the reader does not enter R1 (contrast case 2).
        # all other pointers untouched; |P| unchanged

        return R

    Post: TYPE_AT(R, p) == B, UNCHANGED.

    Cases 2 and 4 differ only in the position R1 is grafted into, and that
    single difference decides everything:
        argument position -> type becomes [tr1], reader ENTERS  R1, adopt Pr
        function position -> type stays B,       reader REMAINS outside, drop Pr

    Convenience wrapper for the common move "arrive at B from a plain entity
    A" (the omitted case 3). Expose this in the interface; it is not a
    separate primitive:

    procedure CONTRACT_INCOMING(R, p, arrow = (A, B)):
        require LEGAL_CONTRACTION_ARRIVE(R, p, arrow)      # [t1] == B
        return CONTRACT_INCOMING_READING(R, p, TRIVIAL_READING(A), B)


5d. Case 5  —  REMOVED (kept as a possible future enhancement)
---------------------------------------------------------------

Removed so that every step is local (reading_desc §3b, §4.2d). It was the only
operation taking two READINGS and no pointer:

    function JOIN_READINGS(R1, R2) -> Reading:        # NOT part of the calculus
        t = App(R1.term, R2.term)                     # [t] == [R2.term]
        return Reading(graph = App-node(R1.graph, R2.graph),
                       term = t, pointers = R2.pointers)   # P1 discarded

Removing it costs no expressive power: cases 2 and 4 already graft a saved
reading's whole term at a pointer, which yields every application shape
(reading_desc §9.1). To build app(t1, t2) with both sides compound, save t2 as
a reading and graft it at a pointer — no joining needed.

If reinstated, the open questions were whether it produces a new reading or
mutates one in place, and whether R1 == R2 is permitted.


6. Reflection  —  fork attention over a shared subject
-------------------------------------------------------

    procedure REFLECT(R, p, B: Entity, C: Entity):
        require LEGAL_REFLECTION(R, p, B, C)

        tA = SUBTERM_AT(R, p)                    # [tA] == A

        b = fresh Var of type B                  # [A] fresh vars, not
        c = fresh Var of type C                  #     entity constants

        # tA occurs twice in the TERM but exists once in the GRAPH.
        fan = NEW_SHARING_FANIN(over = G(tA))    # cf. _Compiler._fan_in :
                                                 #   arity-3 INTERNAL fan
                                                 #   marked on OFFSET wire
        left  = App(tA, b)                       # attaches to fan.grey
        right = App(tA, c)                       # attaches to fan.black
        t2    = App(left, right)

        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=p, old=G(tA), new=G(t2))
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, tA, t2)

        pB = POINTER_TO_EDGE_AT(fan, port="grey")    # upper LEFT  aux port
        pC = POINTER_TO_EDGE_AT(fan, port="black")   # upper RIGHT aux port

        R.pointers = (R.pointers \ {p}) union {pB, pC}
        return R

    Post: TYPE_AT(R, pB) == B  and  TYPE_AT(R, pC) == C
          exactly one G(tA) exists in the graph            # invariant I5

    Why OFFSET matters: the sharing fan-in must sit on the OFFSET wire while
    the syntactic lambda/application fans sit on COMMAND. Same wire => the two
    fans meet with equal `main` => rule R1 ANNIHILATE (the shared subject is
    consumed once — wrong). Different wires => rule R4 DUPLICATE (the subject
    is copied into each context — right).
    See skill `understand-bus-optimal-reduction` §3.


6b. Ontologies  —  models in which the reading is valid
--------------------------------------------------------

See reading_desc §7. A reading records what the user did; an ontology is a
term WITH abstractions whose reduction replays the reading:

    contraction  <->  R1 fires (fan/fan same wire, annihilate)
    reflection   <->  R4 fires (fan/fan different wires, duplicate)

Every reading step: first UPDATE (filter + advance), then ENRICH.

    function QUESTIONS(A: Entity) -> set[Question]:
        # already implemented: widgets.py:_record_contains_iri does exactly
        #   isinstance(term, Abs) and term.var.iri == A
        return { q in QStore : TYPE_OF(q) == A }        # [(lam a) t] = [a]


6b.1 Enrichment
---------------

    procedure ENRICH_ALL(onts: set[Ontology]):
        for each o in onts:                    # iterate a SNAPSHOT
            ENRICH(o, o.pointers, onts)

    procedure ENRICH(o, P, onts):
        p = HEAD(P)
        if p == null: return
        let p designate subterm tp of o.term

        ENRICH(o, P - p, onts)                 # the remaining pointers

        # (a) tp is a variable in FUNCTION position of an application
        if o.term has subterm App(tp, _) and tp is a Var:
            for each q in QUESTIONS(TYPE_OF(tp)):
                o2  = o with o.graph's G(tp) replaced by G(q)
                pq  = pointer to q in o2
                o2.pointers = (P - p) union {pq}
                onts.add(o2)
                ENRICH(o2, P - p, onts)

        # (b) tp is in ARGUMENT position, function side t1 a variable
        if o.term has subterm App(t1, tp) and t1 is a Var:
            for each q in QUESTIONS(TYPE_OF(t1)):
                o2 = o with o.graph's G(t1) replaced by G(q)
                o2.pointers = P                # pointer set unchanged
                onts.add(o2)
                ENRICH(o2, P - p, onts)

    TERMINATION: every recursive call passes P - p, and a newly created
    ontology is enriched only for the REMAINING pointers — a question is never
    substituted inside a question introduced in the same enrichment. Depth is
    bounded by |P|.

    Note the asymmetry: (a) replaces p by pq (the question becomes a new
    position); (b) leaves P alone. This follows the argument/function principle
    of reading_desc §5.


6b.2 Initialisation
-------------------

    function INIT_ONTS_FROM_ENTITY(a: Var) -> set[Ontology]:
        o = Ontology(graph=compile_term(a), term=a,
                     pointers={ptr to the edge G(a)})
        onts = {o}; ENRICH_ALL(onts); return onts

    function INIT_ONTS_FROM_SAVED(R1: Reading) -> set[Ontology]:
        onts = COPY_OF(R1.ontologies); ENRICH_ALL(onts); return onts


6b.3 Update on contraction case 1   (arrow A -> B at pointer p)
----------------------------------------------------------------

    procedure ONTS_AFTER_CONTRACT_1(R, p, arrow = (A, B)):
        newOnts = {}
        for each o in R.ontologies:
            p1 = CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, p)        # see INVARIANT below
            if p1 == null: continue

            # p1 must point at ta inside an application App(ta, tb)
            if o.term has subterm App(ta, tb) with p1 designating ta
               and TYPE_OF(ta) == A and TYPE_OF(tb) == B:
                if R1_APPLICABLE(o.graph, p1):      # i.e. ta is an abstraction
                    pb = pointer to tb
                    APPLY_R1(o.graph, p1)
                    BOOKKEEP(o.graph)               # rules R2, R3, R5, R6
                    o2 = o with pointers = (o.pointers - {p1}) union {pb}
                    newOnts.add(o2)

        R.ontologies = newOnts                      # REFUTATION: see 6b.5
        ENRICH_ALL(R.ontologies)

    The pointer moves from the abstraction ta to tb, mirroring the reading,
    where case 1 moves the reader from A to B.


6b.4 Update on reflection   (B -> C at p, p designating ta with [ta] == A)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    procedure ONTS_AFTER_REFLECT(R, p, B, C):
        newOnts = {}
        for each o in R.ontologies:
            p1 = CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, p)
            if p1 == null: continue
            let p1 designate toa

            if toa is an Abs
               and R4_APPLICABLE(o.graph, p1)
               and o.term has subterm App(tb, tc)
                   with TYPE_OF(tb)==B, TYPE_OF(tc)==C, TYPE_OF(toa)==A
               and IS_SHARED_IN_BOTH(o.graph, toa, tb, tc):   # PRECONDITION
                APPLY_R4(o.graph, p1)
                BOOKKEEP(o.graph)
                pB = pointer to upper-LEFT  aux port edge     # fan-in `grey`
                pC = pointer to upper-RIGHT aux port edge     # fan-in `black`
                o2 = o with pointers = (o.pointers - {p1}) union {pB, pC}
                newOnts.add(o2)

        R.ontologies = newOnts
        ENRICH_ALL(R.ontologies)

    IMPLEMENTATION CAVEAT on IS_SHARED_IN_BOTH. Requiring G(toa) to be ALREADY
    shared across tb and tc is a deliberate precondition (reading_desc §7.6),
    and it is STRICTER than the reducer's own applicability test.
    `optimal_lambda/rules.py:rule4_fan_fan_diff` fires whenever two fans meet
    principal-to-principal on DIFFERENT main wires, and its EFFECT is to
    replicate each fan onto the other's grey/black branches — the reducer
    CREATES duplication rather than requiring it. So IS_SHARED_IN_BOTH is an
    extra filter layered on top of `find_redexes`; it must be implemented and
    checked separately, not read off redex detection.


6b.5 Refutation
---------------

Both update procedures REPLACE R.ontologies rather than adding to it: an
ontology in which the required rule does not fire is DISCARDED, being falsified
by what the user just did. The set shrinks by refutation and grows by
enrichment.

    AN EMPTY ONTOLOGY SET IS MEANINGFUL — the reading has no valid model. It is
    a distinguished state and must be surfaced to the user:

        if R.ontologies is empty:
            SIGNAL("this reading has no valid model")

    Note this is deliberately NOT folded into ASSERT_INVARIANTS: an empty
    ontology set is a legitimate (if notable) outcome, not a broken state.


6b.6 The correspondence invariant
---------------------------------

    INVARIANT (reading_desc O12): every pointer p of the reading has exactly one
    counterpart p1 in each ontology, and CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, p) finds it.

    Maintained by construction: enrichment case (a) replaces p by pq, case (b)
    leaves P alone, and each update procedure rewrites pointers in step with the
    reading's own rule. Every ontology procedure depends on this totally, so
    implement CORRESPONDING_POINTER explicitly (e.g. carry a reading-pointer id
    on each ontology pointer) rather than re-deriving it positionally.


6b.7 Update on contraction cases 2 and 4  —  OPEN
--------------------------------------------------

    NOT SPECIFIED (reading_desc §7.8, O10). Cases 2 and 4 graft a saved reading
    R1, which carries its own ontology set, so the two sets must be combined.
    Candidate: cartesian product { graft(o, o1) : o in R.ontologies,
    o1 in R1.ontologies } with pointers following the reading's own rule (case 2
    adopts R1's, case 4 discards them). Alternative: graft only R1's plain term
    and let ENRICH_ALL re-derive questions, discarding R1's inferred models.
    Left open by decision, 2026-07-29.


7. Driver
---------

    procedure RUN_READING():
        choice = ask user: start at entity | resume saved reading

        if choice == start at entity:
            A = user picks an Entity
            R = INIT_FROM_ENTITY(A)
            R.ontologies = INIT_ONTS_FROM_ENTITY(R.term)        # §6b.2
        else:
            R1 = user picks a saved Reading from Store
            p  = user picks a Pointer from R1.pointers
            (R, p) = INIT_FROM_SAVED(R1, p)
            R.ontologies = INIT_ONTS_FROM_SAVED(R1)             # §6b.2

        loop until user stops:
            # every operation acts at one pointer — there is no reading-level
            # branch, since case 5 was removed (see §5d)
            p = user picks a Pointer from R.pointers
            A = TYPE_AT(R, p)

            op = user picks: contraction | reflection

            if op == contraction:
                # what is offered is decided by the type A at the pointer:
                #   outgoing arrows (A -> X)  admit cases 1 and 2 (depart /
                #                                                  descend)
                #   incoming arrows (X -> A)  admit case 4        (arrive)
                target = user picks one of
                     an outgoing Entity B   -> CONTRACT_RELATION(R,p,(A,B))
                                               ONTS_AFTER_CONTRACT_1(R,p,(A,B))
                   | a saved Reading R1     -> CONTRACT_READING(R,p,A,R1)
                                               # ontology update: OPEN (§6b.7)
                   | an incoming Entity X   -> CONTRACT_INCOMING(R,p,(X,A))
                                               # = case 4 at TRIVIAL_READING(X)
                                               # ontology update: OPEN (§6b.7)
                   | a saved Reading R1     -> CONTRACT_INCOMING_READING(
                                                   R,p,R1,A)
                                               # ontology update: OPEN (§6b.7)

                # note: the two saved-reading options differ only in grafting
                # position, hence in whether the reader ENTERS R1 (case 2,
                # adopts Pr) or stays put (case 4, discards Pr). Both are
                # offered at any pointer, because a saved reading has no
                # entity endpoint to match [t1] against (see reading_desc O7)
                # — so the type at p does NOT select between them, unlike
                # case 1 vs case 4 on a plain arrow. The UI must present them
                # as distinct choices.

            else:  # reflection
                (B, C) = user picks two relations out of A       # [A]
                REFLECT(R, p, B, C)
                ONTS_AFTER_REFLECT(R, p, B, C)                  # §6b.4

            ASSERT_INVARIANTS(R)
            if R.ontologies is empty:                           # §6b.5
                SIGNAL("this reading has no valid model")

        optionally: SAVE(R, name)      # makes R available to init-2 and to
                                       # contraction cases 2 and 4. NOT
                                       # optional in effect: saved-reading
                                       # grafting is what makes the calculus
                                       # shape-complete (reading_desc §10.1).
                                       # NOTE: saving must persist ontologies
                                       # too, since init-2 inherits them (§6b.2)


8. Invariant check
------------------

    procedure ASSERT_INVARIANTS(R):
        for p in R.pointers:
            assert DESIGNATES_LIVE_NODE_OR_EDGE(R.graph, p)      # I1
            assert TYPE_AT(R, p) is defined                      # I3
        assert R.pointers is non-empty                           # I6
        assert R.graph.check_integrity() == []      # optimal_lambda.graph
        # width arithmetic is the usual source of silent breakage:
        # R2/R5 widen by +1, R3/R6 narrow by -1.

        # ontology side (§6b.6). NOTE: an EMPTY ontology set is legitimate and
        # is deliberately NOT asserted here — it means "no valid model", which
        # the driver surfaces to the user instead.
        for o in R.ontologies:
            assert o.graph.check_integrity() == []
            for p in R.pointers:
                assert CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, p) != null       # O12


9. Complexity / notes for the implementer
------------------------------------------

  - EVERY operation is LOCAL: each rewrites one subgraph at one pointer. Cost
    is O(size of the grafted material), not O(|G(t)|) — except DEEP_COPY when a
    saved reading is grafted (cases 2, 4), which is O(|G(tr1)|). Choosing
    SHARE_GRAFTED_READING makes that O(1) at the price of mutable saved
    readings.

  - THE READING LAYER never reduces: it only builds terms. THE ONTOLOGY LAYER
    does — every ontology update applies R1 or R4 plus full bookkeeping
    (R2/R3/R5/R6). So `optimal_lambda.normalize` / `rules.py` are load-bearing
    for ontologies even though the reading itself never needs them. This
    supersedes the earlier note that nothing in the construction requires
    reduction (reading_desc O5 concerned the READING only).

  - POINTER STABILITY UNDER REDUCTION is now the central implementation hazard,
    not a hypothetical one. Ontology pointers are held ACROSS R1/R4 and across
    bookkeeping reductions, and `optimal_lambda/rules.py` knows nothing about
    pointers — `_annihilate`, `_capture_ports` and `_finalize` rewire ends
    freely. Either the rules must be extended to carry pointers through, or
    pointers must be re-derived after each reduction from something stable
    (e.g. the SyntaxRole/iri carried on fans). This must be solved before the
    ontology layer can work at all.

  - COST OF THE ONTOLOGY LAYER. Enrichment branches over QUESTIONS(A) at every
    pointer on every step, so the set can grow multiplicatively; refutation
    prunes it, but nothing guarantees the rates balance (reading_desc O11).
    Each surviving ontology also pays a reduction cost per step. Budget for
    a cap plus a deterministic eviction policy.

  - Persistence gap: `kg_store.save_readings_db` stores (name, term) pairs
    only. Neither pointer sets NOR ontology sets are persisted, so init-2 —
    which inherits R1's ontologies (§6b.2) — and contraction case 2 cannot be
    restored across sessions without extending that schema. Questions already
    persist separately (the lambda DB, via term_utils.append_lambda_term).
    See reading_desc O4.
